A||||9||racefan||Timing woes||||1||
Z||000000||racefan||10-06-2001||11:00 AM||||I have this problem getting my 2.5L 4 cyl to start. I have just finished rebuilding it and cannot get it to start. When cranking, it backfires through the carb and exhaust. I double checked to make sure that the timing marks on the crank and cam gears lined up and that the number 1 cylinder was at TDC with both valves closed and lined up the distrubutor to the #1 spark plug wire. Still, it backfires and doesn't start. Is there any other way to make sure that the timing is correct? Any help would be appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||SKIDMARK||10-06-2001||11:34 AM||||Welcome to the forum.<P>Is it possible that you tightened the rockers down too tight? This will hold the valves open and you won't get any compression. If not, then is it possible the wires are mixed up? Just a thought!||||reg||1
Z||000002||racefan||10-06-2001||11:45 AM||||I tightened the rockers one full turn after the pushrod stopped turning. Also, I rechecked the wires and the firing order (1342) and it looks okay.||||reg||1
Z||000003||88CoupeV6||10-06-2001||01:30 PM||||welcome to the forum. I am just bumping this to the top for you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg">   <BR><B>Also 88 Yellow GT</B> :D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||racefan||10-06-2001||03:01 PM||||Thanks. Well, I went out and checked everything again, but it still backfires. Could this be a problem with the head? The head was okay before the rebuild and I didn't do anything with it, but I am grasping at straws now. Is there anyway using a degree wheel or something to check the actual timing? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||2farnorth||10-07-2001||07:58 AM||||You might try advancing and retarding distributor timing to see if it makes any difference.  Retard it about 15 degrees and try it.  If that doesn't work try advancing it 15 degrees  and try it again.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||10-07-2001||08:24 AM||||Are you sure that the plug wires are installed in the correct rotation on the distributor?||||reg||1
Z||000007||racefan||10-07-2001||10:36 AM||||I think I may have a lead on the problem. I did a compression check and found that the compression readings were real low, below 30lbs. I adjusted the valves and that brought it up a bit, but still far below what it should be. I squirted some oil in the cylinders but that didn't change anything so I knew that the rings were good.<BR>I pulled off the head and in checking the new head gasket that I installed, I noticed that it was a lot thinner than the one that was on there, so I went and purchased a thicker, better gasket hoping that it will seat better and bring my readings up. I will try it today and see what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rodrv6||10-07-2001||02:21 PM||||Just a note.  There is no valve adjustment for the 4 cylinder engine.  It is designed to have the rocker arm nuts run all the way down and tightened to 20-24 ft/lbs.  This assumes that the pushrod length is correct.  If the head has been surfaced and is sitting lower on the block, then the pushrod length may be too long.  I think various lengths are available to fix this situation.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000009||racefan||10-08-2001||10:06 AM||||Well, I finally got it running! Thanks for everyones's help. It was as I said in an earlier post a combination of adjusting the valves and the head gasket that I got from NAPA was a little on the thin side. Getting a thicker Fel-Pro one from Pep Boys brought the compression way up and it kick over and started right up.||||reg||1
